      "writtenOn": "2022-02-10T23:52:41Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This pattern seems like it does a lot of unnecessarily repetitive scanning of the power signal lists. In addition, the pwrseq-signal-enum field in the DT binding seems like it introduces an unnecessary level of indirection.\n\nI assume any given platform has a well-defined set of power signals (which may be a virtual wire or GPIO), so rather than having separate lists and needing to scan them, could there be a single list in known order that indicates whether each signal is a VW or GPIO and provides the configuration?\n\nI imagine something like:\n\n    const struct power_signal_info {\n      const char *name;\n      bool is_virtual_wire;\n      union {\n        struct power_signal_info_gpio gpio_info;\n        struct power_signal_info_vw vw_info;\n      };\n    } power_signals[] \u003d {\n      /* Get each required power signal by node name in well-defined order;\n         SLP_S5, SLP_S3, ... */\n    }\n\nThen the driver can index into that array according to the known order of the signals, and required signals can be marked as such in the DT binding.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 167,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 174,
        "endChar": 2
      },
      "revId": "524a8d1e50f3a1374a3cd3403b4406552039b80e",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0c023a5c_c8657458",
        "filename": "zephyr/subsys/ap_pwrseq/x86_non_dsx_common_pwrseq_sm_handler.c",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 174,
      "author": {
        "id": 1116125
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-02-11T00:53:43Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The power signal can generated from GPIO, VW or ADC(Nivviks).\n\nLegacy EC code does have one power_signal_info list in the same order as \"enum power_signal\" defined. I was thinking to do the same at beginning, to use DT_FOREACH_CHILD() go through power signal list nodes, and generate one info list, also define enum values from it.\n\nI read somewhere in zephyr document (couldn\u0027t find it now), that  DT_FOREACH_CHILD() doesn\u0027t not guarantee to process nodes in the order as they put in the dts file. If this is true, enum value might not same as index to list entry, so I switched to current implementation.\n\nIf DT_FOREACH_CHILD() does process nodes in order as nodes showing in device tree, it would be good that we can use enum as index to directly access power signal info. What information do you have for this DT_FOREACH_CHILD()?",
      "parentUuid": "0c4e8074_c05cb691",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 167,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 174,
        "endChar": 2
      },
      "revId": "524a8d1e50f3a1374a3cd3403b4406552039b80e",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b16cbce4_a2124d68",
        "filename": "zephyr/subsys/ap_pwrseq/x86_non_dsx_common_pwrseq_sm_handler.c",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 174,
      "author": {
        "id": 1281040
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-02-11T00:57:34Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It is true that for different generations of the device tree, the order may change, and thus any enum order generated from these nodes should not be relied on to be consistent across builds. However, within a single build, these are of course consistent (because they are using the same device tree output for that build).",
      "parentUuid": "0c023a5c_c8657458",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 167,
